[2026-03-16T03:17:14.378Z] {"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01CXpuXKvHV7yeVTgK4KhC8L","type":"tool_result","content":"     1→# Skill\n     2→\n     3→During this stage you will polish the app to improve its quality, identifying and fixing\n     4→issues around the app's appearance, performance, code structure, and so on.\n     5→\n     6→## Progress Tracking\n     7→\n     8→Polish work is tracked in `docs/plan.md`. Each polish stage has a section in the file with\n     9→a checklist of completed work. Before unpacking subtasks, read `docs/plan.md` (create it\n    10→if it doesn't exist) and check which stages have already been completed.\n    11→\n    12→The format is:\n    13→\n    14→```markdown\n    15→## Polish\n    16→\n    17→### Responsive UI\n    18→- [x] ClientsListPage\n    19→- [x] ClientDetailPage\n    20→- [ ] DealsListPage\n    21→```\n    22→\n    23→When a polish stage is fully complete, mark it done:\n    24→\n    25→```markdown\n    26→### Responsive UI ✓\n    27→```\n    28→\n    29→A stage with `✓` in its heading is skipped during unpacking — no tasks are added for it.\n    30→\n    31→## Unpack Subtasks\n    32→\n    33→Read `docs/plan.md` and check which polish stages still need work. For each incomplete stage,\n    34→add the appropriate tasks as described below. After adding tasks, update `docs/plan.md` with\n    35→the planned items (unchecked). As each subtask completes, check off its entry.\n    36→\n    37→### Accessibility\n    38→\n    39→If the `Accessibility` section in `docs/plan.md` is missing or not marked `✓`, the app needs\n    40→accessibility work. Read `docs/tests.md` to identify all pages, then add ALL page tasks in a\n    41→single `add-task` call — write out every page explicitly, do not use a loop or script:\n    42→\n    43→```bash\n    44→npx tsx /repo/scripts/add-task.ts <<'EOF'\n    45→[\n    46→  { \"skill\": \"skills/tasks/maintain/polishApp.md\", \"app\": \"<AppName>\", \"subtasks\": [\"MakeAccessible<Page1>: Make <Page1> accessible\"] },\n    47→  { \"skill\": \"skills/tasks/maintain/polishApp.md\", \"app\": \"<AppName>\", \"subtasks\": [\"MakeAccessible<Page2>: Make <Page2> accessible\"] }\n    48→]\n    49→EOF\n    50→```\n    51→\n    52→Add or update the `Accessibility` section in `docs/plan.md` with an unchecked entry for each page.\n    53→\n    54→### Responsive UI\n    55→\n    56→If the `Responsive UI` section in `docs/plan.md` is missing or not marked `✓`, the app needs\n    57→responsive work. Read `docs/tests.md` to identify all pages, then add ALL page tasks in a\n    58→single `add-task` call — write out every page explicitly, do not use a loop or script:\n    59→\n    60→```bash\n    61→npx tsx /repo/scripts/add-task.ts <<'EOF'\n    62→[\n    63→  { \"skill\": \"skills/tasks/maintain/polishApp.md\", \"app\": \"<AppName>\", \"subtasks\": [\"MakeResponsive<Page1>: Make <Page1> responsive\"] },\n    64→  { \"skill\": \"skills/tasks/maintain/polishApp.md\", \"app\": \"<AppName>\", \"subtasks\": [\"MakeResponsive<Page2>: Make <Page2> responsive\"] }\n    65→]\n    66→EOF\n    67→```\n    68→\n    69→Add or update the `Responsive UI` section in `docs/plan.md` with an unchecked entry for each page.\n    70→\n    71→### Performance\n    72→\n    73→If the `Performance` section in `docs/plan.md` is missing or not marked `✓`, the app needs\n    74→performance work. Add a single task for the backend API review:\n    75→\n    76→```bash\n    77→npx tsx /repo/scripts/add-task.ts <<'EOF'\n    78→[\n    79→  { \"skill\": \"skills/tasks/maintain/polishApp.md\", \"app\": \"<AppName>\", \"subtasks\": [\"PaginateAPIs: Review and paginate backend APIs that return large datasets\"] }\n    80→]\n    81→EOF\n    82→```\n    83→\n    84→Add or update the `Performance` section in `docs/plan.md` with an unchecked entry for the API review.\n    85→\n    86→## Making a Page Accessible\n    87→\n    88→When working on a `MakeAccessible` subtask, audit the page and its components for accessibility\n    89→issues and fix them. The goal is that all functionality is usable via keyboard alone and that\n    90→screen readers can navigate and understand all content.\n    91→\n    92→After completing the work, check off the page in `docs/plan.md`. If all pages are done, mark the\n    93→section heading with `✓`.\n    94→\n    95→### Semantic HTML\n    96→\n    97→- Use semantic elements (`<nav>`, `<main>`, `<section>`, `<header>`, `<footer>`, `<aside>`) instead\n    98→  of bare `<div>`s for page structure. Each page's content area should be wrapped in `<main>`.\n    99→- Use `<button>` for clickable actions and `<a>` for navigation. Never attach click handlers to\n   100→  `<div>` or `<span>` elements.\n   101→- Use heading elements (`<h1>`–`<h6>`) in proper hierarchical order — one `<h1>` per page, no\n   102→  skipped levels.\n   103→- Use `<ul>`/`<ol>` for lists of items, `<table>` for tabular data.\n   104→\n   105→### Keyboard navigation\n   106→\n   107→- All interactive elements must be reachable via Tab and activatable via Enter/Space.\n   108→- Modals and dialogs must trap focus while open and return focus to the trigger element on close.\n   109→- Dropdown menus and popovers must support Escape to close and arrow keys to navigate options.\n   110→- Visible focus indicators must be present on all focusable elements — never set `outline: none`\n   111→  without providing an alternative focus style.\n   112→\n   113→### ARIA attributes\n   114→\n   115→- Add `aria-label` or `aria-labelledby` to interactive elements that lack visible text labels\n   116→  (icon-only buttons, icon links, search inputs).\n   117→- Use `role=\"dialog\"` and `aria-modal=\"true\"` on modal containers with `aria-labelledby` pointing\n   118→  to the modal title.\n   119→- Use `aria-expanded` on buttons that toggle collapsible sections or dropdowns.\n   120→- Use `aria-current=\"page\"` on the active navigation link in sidebars/navbars.\n   121→- Add `aria-live=\"polite\"` to regions that update dynamically (toast notifications, status messages,\n   122→  loading states).\n   123→- Use `aria-describedby` to associate error messages with their form fields.\n   124→\n   125→### Forms\n   126→\n   127→- Every form input must have an associated `<label>` element (via `htmlFor`) or an `aria-label`.\n   128→- Group related fields with `<fieldset>` and `<legend>` where appropriate.\n   129→- Display validation errors adjacent to the relevant field with `role=\"alert\"` or linked via\n   130→  `aria-describedby`.\n   131→- Required fields should use the `aria-required=\"true\"` attribute.\n   132→\n   133→### Color and contrast\n   134→\n   135→- Text must meet WCAG AA contrast ratios: 4.5:1 for normal text, 3:1 for large text (18px+ bold\n   136→  or 24px+ regular).\n   137→- Never convey information through color alone — pair colors with icons, text labels, or patterns\n   138→  (e.g., status badges should include text, not just a colored dot).\n   139→- Focus indicators must have at least 3:1 contrast against adjacent colors.\n   140→\n   141→### Images and media\n   142→\n   143→- All `<img>` elements must have an `alt` attribute. Decorative images use `alt=\"\"`.\n   144→- Icons used as the sole content of a button or link need `aria-label` on the parent element or\n   145→  `aria-hidden=\"true\"` on the icon with a visually hidden text label alongside it.\n   146→\n   147→## Making a Page Responsive\n   148→\n   149→When working on a `MakeResponsive` subtask, update the page and its components so the layout adapts\n   150→cleanly as the viewport shrinks from desktop down to mobile widths. The goal is that all content\n   151→remains usable and readable — no horizontal scrolling, no overlapping elements, no truncated\n   152→controls.\n   153→\n   154→After completing the work, check off the page in `docs/plan.md`. If all pages are done, mark the\n   155→section heading with `✓`.\n   156→\n   157→### Implementation approach\n   158→\n   159→Use **Tailwind responsive utility classes** (max-width variants) as the primary tool. Only fall\n   160→back to raw CSS media queries in `index.css` when Tailwind utilities cannot express the rule\n   161→(e.g., hiding table columns rendered from mapped data, or switching `<tr>` display modes).\n   162→\n   163→**Do not mix min-width and max-width variants.** Use `max-*:` variants consistently so styles\n   164→degrade from desktop down:\n   165→- `max-lg:` — max-width 1024px\n   166→- `max-md:` — max-width 768px\n   167→- `max-sm:` — max-width 640px\n   168→\n   169→When raw CSS is needed (e.g., table column hiding via nth-child or tag selectors), use `@media\n   170→(max-width: ...)` at 1024px, 768px, and 480px. The 480px CSS breakpoint covers the gap below\n   171→Tailwind's 640px `max-sm:`.\n   172→\n   173→Do not use JavaScript-based layout switching. Do not add a hamburger menu or mobile navigation\n   174→unless the app already has one — the sidebar stays fixed.\n   175→\n   176→### Layout adjustments\n   177→\n   178→Apply these patterns at the Tailwind breakpoints:\n   179→\n   180→- **Page padding**: `p-6 max-sm:p-3` on the outermost page wrapper.\n   181→- **Multi-column grids** (detail page bodies, metadata grids): start with the desktop column\n   182→  count and collapse at each breakpoint.\n   183→  - Two-column body: `grid grid-cols-2 max-md:grid-cols-1 gap-4`\n   184→  - Four-column metadata: `grid grid-cols-4 max-md:grid-cols-2 max-sm:grid-cols-1 gap-4`\n   185→- **Section card padding**: `p-4 max-sm:p-3` or `px-5 max-sm:px-3`.\n   186→- **Form layouts** with side-by-side fields: `grid grid-cols-2 max-sm:grid-cols-1 gap-4`.\n   187→- **Typography scaling**: reduce heading sizes at `max-sm:`.\n   188→  - Page titles: `text-[24px] max-sm:text-[20px]`\n   189→  - Detail headings: `text-[18px] max-sm:text-[16px]`\n   190→- **Button text**: on narrow viewports, hide button labels and keep only icons:\n   191→  `<span className=\"max-sm:hidden\">Label</span>`\n   192→- **Horizontal button rows** and action bars: use `flex-wrap` so they wrap naturally.\n   193→- **Modals and dialogs**: constrain with `max-sm:max-w-[calc(100%-24px)]` and ensure\n   194→  `overflow-y-auto` for tall content.\n   195→\n   196→### Data density in lists and tables\n   197→\n   198→Lists and tables are the most important thing to get right. On wide viewports they can show many\n   199→columns, but on narrow viewports they must progressively hide less-important columns.\n   200→\n   201→- Identify which columns are essential (e.g., name, status) vs secondary (e.g., created date,\n   202→  assigned user, tags).\n   203→- **Column hiding**: Use Tailwind `max-lg:hidden` and `max-md:hidden` directly on `<th>`/`<td>`\n   204→  elements when the table is rendered with explicit column elements. When columns are generated\n   205→  from mapped data or use CSS Grid, define named classes in `index.css` with `@media` rules.\n   206→- **Card mode at narrowest viewport**: At 480px (via `@media` in `index.css`), hide the table\n   207→  header and switch rows to `display: flex; flex-wrap: wrap` so each row reads as a card with\n   208→  key fields only.\n   209→- Ensure sort/filter controls remain accessible — use `flex-wrap` on filter bars so controls\n   210→  wrap naturally on narrow viewports.\n   211→\n   212→### Card-based lists\n   213→\n   214→When the page uses cards instead of a table (e.g., task lists), adjust information density within\n   215→each card at breakpoints:\n   216→\n   217→- At `max-lg:` hide secondary metadata (avatars, role text).\n   218→- At `max-md:` relocate metadata that was in a side column to an inline row below the title\n   219→  using `hidden max-md:flex`.\n   220→- At `max-sm:` consolidate remaining metadata into a single compact row using\n   221→  `hidden max-sm:flex`.\n   222→\n   223→## Paginating Backend APIs\n   224→\n   225→When working on a `PaginateAPIs` subtask, review every backend API function (Netlify function or\n   226→equivalent) that returns a list of records. Any endpoint that could return an unbounded or large\n   227→number of rows must be updated to limit the data returned per request.\n   228→\n   229→After completing the work, check off the entry in `docs/plan.md`. If all performance items are\n   230→done, mark the section heading with `✓`.\n   231→\n   232→### Identifying endpoints to fix\n   233→\n   234→Read all backend function files and find every endpoint that queries a list of records (e.g.,\n   235→`SELECT * FROM ...` without a `LIMIT`, or an ORM call like `.findMany()` / `.select()` that\n   236→returns all matching rows). Endpoints that return a single record by ID or a small fixed set\n   237→(e.g., enum values, current user) can be skipped.\n   238→\n   239→### Choosing a strategy\n   240→\n   241→Pick the approach that fits how the frontend consumes the data:\n   242→\n   243→- **Cursor-based pagination** (preferred for lists the user scrolls through): return a `cursor`\n   244→  (typically the last row's ID or sort key) and a `hasMore` flag. The frontend requests the next\n   245→  page by passing the cursor back. This avoids the count query and handles concurrent inserts\n   246→  gracefully.\n   247→- **Offset/limit pagination** (acceptable when the UI shows page numbers): return `items`,\n   248→  `total`, `page`, and `pageSize`. Use `LIMIT` and `OFFSET` in the query.\n   249→- **Chunked loading** (for data that must all be present client-side, e.g., chart data, reports):\n   250→  if the total dataset can grow large but the frontend needs it all, break it into smaller\n   251→  sequential requests by date range or category rather than loading everything in one call.\n   252→\n   253→### Implementation guidelines\n   254→\n   255→- Default page size should be reasonable for the data type — typically 20–50 rows for table/list\n   256→  views, up to 100 for lightweight records.\n   257→- Accept `pageSize` / `limit` as a query parameter but enforce a maximum (e.g., 100) to prevent\n   258→  abuse.\n   259→- Always apply an `ORDER BY` to ensure stable pagination ordering.\n   260→- Update the corresponding frontend code to pass pagination parameters and handle paginated\n   261→  responses (load-more buttons, infinite scroll, or page controls as appropriate for the UI).\n   262→- Update any frontend code that assumes all records are returned at once (e.g., client-side\n   263→  filtering/sorting across the full dataset) — either move that logic server-side or fetch all\n   264→  pages when needed for search/filter.\n   265→"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"6c9eec15-4aa7-4280-9f57-4071abbe9889","uuid":"f34a72b5-3bf3-4d75-97f2-d5be5be58449","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/repo/skills/tasks/maintain/polishApp.md","content":"# Skill\n\nDuring this stage you will polish the app to improve its quality, identifying and fixing\nissues around the app's appearance, performance, code structure, and so on.\n\n## Progress Tracking\n\nPolish work is tracked in `docs/plan.md`. Each polish stage has a section in the file with\na checklist of completed work. Before unpacking subtasks, read `docs/plan.md` (create it\nif it doesn't exist) and check which stages have already been completed.\n\nThe format is:\n\n```markdown\n## Polish\n\n### Responsive UI\n- [x] ClientsListPage\n- [x] ClientDetailPage\n- [ ] DealsListPage\n```\n\nWhen a polish stage is fully complete, mark it done:\n\n```markdown\n### Responsive UI ✓\n```\n\nA stage with `✓` in its heading is skipped during unpacking — no tasks are added for it.\n\n## Unpack Subtasks\n\nRead `docs/plan.md` and check which polish stages still need work. For each incomplete stage,\nadd the appropriate tasks as described below. After adding tasks, update `docs/plan.md` with\nthe planned items (unchecked). As each subtask completes, check off its entry.\n\n### Accessibility\n\nIf the `Accessibility` section in `docs/plan.md` is missing or not marked `✓`, the app needs\naccessibility work. Read `docs/tests.md` to identify all pages, then add ALL page tasks in a\nsingle `add-task` call — write out every page explicitly, do not use a loop or script:\n\n```bash\nnpx tsx /repo/scripts/add-task.ts <<'EOF'\n[\n  { \"skill\": \"skills/tasks/maintain/polishApp.md\", \"app\": \"<AppName>\", \"subtasks\": [\"MakeAccessible<Page1>: Make <Page1> accessible\"] },\n  { \"skill\": \"skills/tasks/maintain/polishApp.md\", \"app\": \"<AppName>\", \"subtasks\": [\"MakeAccessible<Page2>: Make <Page2> accessible\"] }\n]\nEOF\n```\n\nAdd or update the `Accessibility` section in `docs/plan.md` with an unchecked entry for each page.\n\n### Responsive UI\n\nIf the `Responsive UI` section in `docs/plan.md` is missing or not marked `✓`, the app needs\nresponsive work. Read `docs/tests.md` to identify all pages, then add ALL page tasks in a\nsingle `add-task` call — write out every page explicitly, do not use a loop or script:\n\n```bash\nnpx tsx /repo/scripts/add-task.ts <<'EOF'\n[\n  { \"skill\": \"skills/tasks/maintain/polishApp.md\", \"app\": \"<AppName>\", \"subtasks\": [\"MakeResponsive<Page1>: Make <Page1> responsive\"] },\n  { \"skill\": \"skills/tasks/maintain/polishApp.md\", \"app\": \"<AppName>\", \"subtasks\": [\"MakeResponsive<Page2>: Make <Page2> responsive\"] }\n]\nEOF\n```\n\nAdd or update the `Responsive UI` section in `docs/plan.md` with an unchecked entry for each page.\n\n### Performance\n\nIf the `Performance` section in `docs/plan.md` is missing or not marked `✓`, the app needs\nperformance work. Add a single task for the backend API review:\n\n```bash\nnpx tsx /repo/scripts/add-task.ts <<'EOF'\n[\n  { \"skill\": \"skills/tasks/maintain/polishApp.md\", \"app\": \"<AppName>\", \"subtasks\": [\"PaginateAPIs: Review and paginate backend APIs that return large datasets\"] }\n]\nEOF\n```\n\nAdd or update the `Performance` section in `docs/plan.md` with an unchecked entry for the API review.\n\n## Making a Page Accessible\n\nWhen working on a `MakeAccessible` subtask, audit the page and its components for accessibility\nissues and fix them. The goal is that all functionality is usable via keyboard alone and that\nscreen readers can navigate and understand all content.\n\nAfter completing the work, check off the page in `docs/plan.md`. If all pages are done, mark the\nsection heading with `✓`.\n\n### Semantic HTML\n\n- Use semantic elements (`<nav>`, `<main>`, `<section>`, `<header>`, `<footer>`, `<aside>`) instead\n  of bare `<div>`s for page structure. Each page's content area should be wrapped in `<main>`.\n- Use `<button>` for clickable actions and `<a>` for navigation. Never attach click handlers to\n  `<div>` or `<span>` elements.\n- Use heading elements (`<h1>`–`<h6>`) in proper hierarchical order — one `<h1>` per page, no\n  skipped levels.\n- Use `<ul>`/`<ol>` for lists of items, `<table>` for tabular data.\n\n### Keyboard navigation\n\n- All interactive elements must be reachable via Tab and activatable via Enter/Space.\n- Modals and dialogs must trap focus while open and return focus to the trigger element on close.\n- Dropdown menus and popovers must support Escape to close and arrow keys to navigate options.\n- Visible focus indicators must be present on all focusable elements — never set `outline: none`\n  without providing an alternative focus style.\n\n### ARIA attributes\n\n- Add `aria-label` or `aria-labelledby` to interactive elements that lack visible text labels\n  (icon-only buttons, icon links, search inputs).\n- Use `role=\"dialog\"` and `aria-modal=\"true\"` on modal containers with `aria-labelledby` pointing\n  to the modal title.\n- Use `aria-expanded` on buttons that toggle collapsible sections or dropdowns.\n- Use `aria-current=\"page\"` on the active navigation link in sidebars/navbars.\n- Add `aria-live=\"polite\"` to regions that update dynamically (toast notifications, status messages,\n  loading states).\n- Use `aria-describedby` to associate error messages with their form fields.\n\n### Forms\n\n- Every form input must have an associated `<label>` element (via `htmlFor`) or an `aria-label`.\n- Group related fields with `<fieldset>` and `<legend>` where appropriate.\n- Display validation errors adjacent to the relevant field with `role=\"alert\"` or linked via\n  `aria-describedby`.\n- Required fields should use the `aria-required=\"true\"` attribute.\n\n### Color and contrast\n\n- Text must meet WCAG AA contrast ratios: 4.5:1 for normal text, 3:1 for large text (18px+ bold\n  or 24px+ regular).\n- Never convey information through color alone — pair colors with icons, text labels, or patterns\n  (e.g., status badges should include text, not just a colored dot).\n- Focus indicators must have at least 3:1 contrast against adjacent colors.\n\n### Images and media\n\n- All `<img>` elements must have an `alt` attribute. Decorative images use `alt=\"\"`.\n- Icons used as the sole content of a button or link need `aria-label` on the parent element or\n  `aria-hidden=\"true\"` on the icon with a visually hidden text label alongside it.\n\n## Making a Page Responsive\n\nWhen working on a `MakeResponsive` subtask, update the page and its components so the layout adapts\ncleanly as the viewport shrinks from desktop down to mobile widths. The goal is that all content\nremains usable and readable — no horizontal scrolling, no overlapping elements, no truncated\ncontrols.\n\nAfter completing the work, check off the page in `docs/plan.md`. If all pages are done, mark the\nsection heading with `✓`.\n\n### Implementation approach\n\nUse **Tailwind responsive utility classes** (max-width variants) as the primary tool. Only fall\nback to raw CSS media queries in `index.css` when Tailwind utilities cannot express the rule\n(e.g., hiding table columns rendered from mapped data, or switching `<tr>` display modes).\n\n**Do not mix min-width and max-width variants.** Use `max-*:` variants consistently so styles\ndegrade from desktop down:\n- `max-lg:` — max-width 1024px\n- `max-md:` — max-width 768px\n- `max-sm:` — max-width 640px\n\nWhen raw CSS is needed (e.g., table column hiding via nth-child or tag selectors), use `@media\n(max-width: ...)` at 1024px, 768px, and 480px. The 480px CSS breakpoint covers the gap below\nTailwind's 640px `max-sm:`.\n\nDo not use JavaScript-based layout switching. Do not add a hamburger menu or mobile navigation\nunless the app already has one — the sidebar stays fixed.\n\n### Layout adjustments\n\nApply these patterns at the Tailwind breakpoints:\n\n- **Page padding**: `p-6 max-sm:p-3` on the outermost page wrapper.\n- **Multi-column grids** (detail page bodies, metadata grids): start with the desktop column\n  count and collapse at each breakpoint.\n  - Two-column body: `grid grid-cols-2 max-md:grid-cols-1 gap-4`\n  - Four-column metadata: `grid grid-cols-4 max-md:grid-cols-2 max-sm:grid-cols-1 gap-4`\n- **Section card padding**: `p-4 max-sm:p-3` or `px-5 max-sm:px-3`.\n- **Form layouts** with side-by-side fields: `grid grid-cols-2 max-sm:grid-cols-1 gap-4`.\n- **Typography scaling**: reduce heading sizes at `max-sm:`.\n  - Page titles: `text-[24px] max-sm:text-[20px]`\n  - Detail headings: `text-[18px] max-sm:text-[16px]`\n- **Button text**: on narrow viewports, hide button labels and keep only icons:\n  `<span className=\"max-sm:hidden\">Label</span>`\n- **Horizontal button rows** and action bars: use `flex-wrap` so they wrap naturally.\n- **Modals and dialogs**: constrain with `max-sm:max-w-[calc(100%-24px)]` and ensure\n  `overflow-y-auto` for tall content.\n\n### Data density in lists and tables\n\nLists and tables are the most important thing to get right. On wide viewports they can show many\ncolumns, but on narrow viewports they must progressively hide less-important columns.\n\n- Identify which columns are essential (e.g., name, status) vs secondary (e.g., created date,\n  assigned user, tags).\n- **Column hiding**: Use Tailwind `max-lg:hidden` and `max-md:hidden` directly on `<th>`/`<td>`\n  elements when the table is rendered with explicit column elements. When columns are generated\n  from mapped data or use CSS Grid, define named classes in `index.css` with `@media` rules.\n- **Card mode at narrowest viewport**: At 480px (via `@media` in `index.css`), hide the table\n  header and switch rows to `display: flex; flex-wrap: wrap` so each row reads as a card with\n  key fields only.\n- Ensure sort/filter controls remain accessible — use `flex-wrap` on filter bars so controls\n  wrap naturally on narrow viewports.\n\n### Card-based lists\n\nWhen the page uses cards instead of a table (e.g., task lists), adjust information density within\neach card at breakpoints:\n\n- At `max-lg:` hide secondary metadata (avatars, role text).\n- At `max-md:` relocate metadata that was in a side column to an inline row below the title\n  using `hidden max-md:flex`.\n- At `max-sm:` consolidate remaining metadata into a single compact row using\n  `hidden max-sm:flex`.\n\n## Paginating Backend APIs\n\nWhen working on a `PaginateAPIs` subtask, review every backend API function (Netlify function or\nequivalent) that returns a list of records. Any endpoint that could return an unbounded or large\nnumber of rows must be updated to limit the data returned per request.\n\nAfter completing the work, check off the entry in `docs/plan.md`. If all performance items are\ndone, mark the section heading with `✓`.\n\n### Identifying endpoints to fix\n\nRead all backend function files and find every endpoint that queries a list of records (e.g.,\n`SELECT * FROM ...` without a `LIMIT`, or an ORM call like `.findMany()` / `.select()` that\nreturns all matching rows). Endpoints that return a single record by ID or a small fixed set\n(e.g., enum values, current user) can be skipped.\n\n### Choosing a strategy\n\nPick the approach that fits how the frontend consumes the data:\n\n- **Cursor-based pagination** (preferred for lists the user scrolls through): return a `cursor`\n  (typically the last row's ID or sort key) and a `hasMore` flag. The frontend requests the next\n  page by passing the cursor back. This avoids the count query and handles concurrent inserts\n  gracefully.\n- **Offset/limit pagination** (acceptable when the UI shows page numbers): return `items`,\n  `total`, `page`, and `pageSize`. Use `LIMIT` and `OFFSET` in the query.\n- **Chunked loading** (for data that must all be present client-side, e.g., chart data, reports):\n  if the total dataset can grow large but the frontend needs it all, break it into smaller\n  sequential requests by date range or category rather than loading everything in one call.\n\n### Implementation guidelines\n\n- Default page size should be reasonable for the data type — typically 20–50 rows for table/list\n  views, up to 100 for lightweight records.\n- Accept `pageSize` / `limit` as a query parameter but enforce a maximum (e.g., 100) to prevent\n  abuse.\n- Always apply an `ORDER BY` to ensure stable pagination ordering.\n- Update the corresponding frontend code to pass pagination parameters and handle paginated\n  responses (load-more buttons, infinite scroll, or page controls as appropriate for the UI).\n- Update any frontend code that assumes all records are returned at once (e.g., client-side\n  filtering/sorting across the full dataset) — either move that logic server-side or fetch all\n  pages when needed for search/filter.\n","numLines":265,"startLine":1,"totalLines":265}}}
[2026-03-16T03:17:14.382Z] {"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_0126kXNnj3VVCyPnJhm5Y65x","type":"tool_result","content":"     1→# Overview\n     2→\n     3→This repository is a platform for agentic app building. You will build and/or maintain one or more\n     4→web apps according to a set of skills and guidelines which will evolve as you get better at building apps.\n     5→\n     6→Key directories:\n     7→\n     8→* `apps`: Has one subdirectory for each app that has been built or has been specified and still needs to be built.\n     9→* `skills/messages`: Skills for responding to messages from the user (e.g. bug reports, log analysis).\n    10→* `skills/tasks`: Skills for performing tasks. See `skills/AGENTS.md` for details.\n    11→* `reports`: Final generated reports from log analysis (see `skills/review/`).\n    12→* `report-data`: Intermediate analysis data used during report generation.\n    13→* `tasks`: The task queue (`tasks-<containerName>.json`) managed by scripts in `scripts/`.\n    14→* `logs`: Log files from work that has been performed. `worker-current.log` is the log for\n    15→  the work currently being done.\n    16→\n    17→You are running within a container and can run test suites for applications and connect to various\n    18→external services using instructions in the relevant skill files.\n    19→\n    20→**CRITICAL — FIRST THING YOU DO when responding to a user message:**\n    21→\n    22→1. **BEFORE doing ANY work**, list the files in `skills/messages/` and read any skill\n    23→   whose name matches the user's request.\n    24→2. If a matching skill exists, follow it exactly.\n    25→3. Only if no matching skill exists should you proceed on your own — and then write a new\n    26→   skill document in `skills/messages/` afterward so you handle it correctly next time.\n    27→\n    28→Do NOT run git commands, search code, or take any other action until you have checked for a\n    29→matching skill. This is the highest-priority rule when handling user messages.\n    30→\n    31→Do NOT modify this AGENTS.md file unless the user explicitly instructs you to.\n    32→All domain-specific guidance belongs in skill files under `skills/`.\n    33→\n    34→## Worker Execution\n    35→\n    36→The worker processes tasks from `tasks/tasks-<containerName>.json` sequentially. For each task,\n    37→it passes all subtasks to Claude as a single prompt and checks for a `<DONE>` signal.\n    38→\n    39→- If `<DONE>` is signaled: the task is dequeued and the worker moves to the next task.\n    40→- If `<DONE>` is NOT signaled: the task is retried (up to 3 times before being skipped).\n    41→\n    42→The worker commits changes after each task automatically. Focus on completing the\n    43→subtasks — do not worry about committing or exiting.\n    44→\n    45→When you have completed ALL subtasks in your task, output `<DONE>` to signal completion.\n    46→\n    47→## Task System\n    48→\n    49→Work is managed through a JSON task queue at `tasks/tasks-<containerName>.json`. The file contains an object\n    50→with a `tasks` array. Each task has a `skill`, an array of `subtasks` (description strings),\n    51→a `timestamp`, and an optional `app` name:\n    52→\n    53→```json\n    54→{\n    55→  \"tasks\": [\n    56→    {\n    57→      \"skill\": \"skills/tasks/maintain/checkDirectives.md\",\n    58→      \"subtasks\": [\n    59→        \"CheckTestSpecAuth: Check testSpec.md directive violations in Authentication\",\n    60→        \"CheckComponentsAuth: Check writeApp.md directive violations in Authentication\",\n    61→        \"CheckTestsAuth: Check writeTests.md directive violations in Authentication\"\n    62→      ],\n    63→      \"timestamp\": \"2026-02-20T00:00:00.000Z\",\n    64→      \"app\": \"sales-crm\"\n    65→    }\n    66→  ]\n    67→}\n    68→```\n    69→\n    70→The agent NEVER reads or writes task files directly. Instead, use `add-task` via stdin heredoc:\n    71→\n    72→```bash\n    73→npx tsx /repo/scripts/add-task.ts <<'EOF'\n    74→[\n    75→  { \"skill\": \"skills/tasks/build/writeApp.md\", \"app\": \"SalesCRM\", \"subtasks\": [\"SetupApp: Setup the app\", \"DesignDatabase: Design the database\"] },\n    76→  { \"skill\": \"skills/tasks/build/writeTests.md\", \"app\": \"SalesCRM\", \"subtasks\": [\"WriteTestAuth: Write test for Auth\"] }\n    77→]\n    78→EOF\n    79→```\n    80→\n    81→Tasks are inserted at the front of the queue in the order listed (first element = first to run).\n    82→Each task object must have `skill` and `subtasks`. `app` is optional.\n    83→\n    84→**CRITICAL: `add-task` usage rules**\n    85→\n    86→- When unpacking tasks, put ALL new tasks in a **single `add-task` call**. Do NOT call\n    87→  `add-task` multiple times.\n    88→- Write out every task explicitly in the JSON array. Do NOT use loops, scripts, or code\n    89→  to generate tasks programmatically.\n    90→- All subtasks in a task share the same skill. Group related subtasks together — for example,\n    91→  all checks for a single page go in one task.\n    92→- When a skill needs to \"unpack\" into sub-tasks, use `add-task` to insert them at the\n    93→  front of the queue.\n    94→\n    95→**CRITICAL: Task scope rules**\n    96→\n    97→- You MUST only work on subtasks that were assigned to you in your current prompt. Your assigned\n    98→  subtasks are the ones passed to you by the worker — nothing else.\n    99→- When you add new tasks via `add-task`, you MUST NOT start working on those tasks.\n   100→  They will be picked up by a future worker iteration.\n   101→- If you have completed all of your assigned subtasks and the only remaining work is tasks you\n   102→  added to the queue, you are DONE. Commit your changes and output `<DONE>` immediately.\n   103→  Do NOT continue working on the newly queued tasks.\n   104→- Adding tasks to the queue is NOT the same as doing those tasks. Add them and stop.\n   105→\n   106→## Tech Stack\n   107→\n   108→- **Vite** for build tooling\n   109→- **TypeScript** for all source code (strict mode)\n   110→- **React 18** for UI (single-page application)\n   111→- **Redux** for client side state management\n   112→- **shadcn/ui** for components\n   113→- **Neon** for database backend (Postgres).\n   114→- **Netlify Functions** for any backend/serverless functions. Place functions in `netlify/functions/`. Use `netlify dev` for local development.\n   115→- **File Storage** use UploadThing with the provided token for any file storage.\n   116→\n   117→## Database\n   118→\n   119→All database accesses must happen in backend Netlify functions. Netlify functions should be focused\n   120→and operate on specific parts of the database corresponding to the needs of one or more specific\n   121→frontend components.\n   122→\n   123→## Running Tests\n   124→\n   125→You MUST read `skills/tasks/build/testing.md` and precisely follow its instructions when running\n   126→tests and debugging test failures.\n   127→\n   128→## Quality Gates\n   129→\n   130→Before each commit, run `npm run check` (see `skills/scripts/check.md`). Do not commit\n   131→code that fails typecheck or lint.\n   132→\n   133→## Commits\n   134→\n   135→The worker commits automatically after each iteration. Do not commit manually unless\n   136→explicitly asked to by a skill or prompt.\n   137→"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"6c9eec15-4aa7-4280-9f57-4071abbe9889","uuid":"6b25e042-54d8-47d7-98e8-69350a732491","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/repo/AGENTS.md","content":"# Overview\n\nThis repository is a platform for agentic app building. You will build and/or maintain one or more\nweb apps according to a set of skills and guidelines which will evolve as you get better at building apps.\n\nKey directories:\n\n* `apps`: Has one subdirectory for each app that has been built or has been specified and still needs to be built.\n* `skills/messages`: Skills for responding to messages from the user (e.g. bug reports, log analysis).\n* `skills/tasks`: Skills for performing tasks. See `skills/AGENTS.md` for details.\n* `reports`: Final generated reports from log analysis (see `skills/review/`).\n* `report-data`: Intermediate analysis data used during report generation.\n* `tasks`: The task queue (`tasks-<containerName>.json`) managed by scripts in `scripts/`.\n* `logs`: Log files from work that has been performed. `worker-current.log` is the log for\n  the work currently being done.\n\nYou are running within a container and can run test suites for applications and connect to various\nexternal services using instructions in the relevant skill files.\n\n**CRITICAL — FIRST THING YOU DO when responding to a user message:**\n\n1. **BEFORE doing ANY work**, list the files in `skills/messages/` and read any skill\n   whose name matches the user's request.\n2. If a matching skill exists, follow it exactly.\n3. Only if no matching skill exists should you proceed on your own — and then write a new\n   skill document in `skills/messages/` afterward so you handle it correctly next time.\n\nDo NOT run git commands, search code, or take any other action until you have checked for a\nmatching skill. This is the highest-priority rule when handling user messages.\n\nDo NOT modify this AGENTS.md file unless the user explicitly instructs you to.\nAll domain-specific guidance belongs in skill files under `skills/`.\n\n## Worker Execution\n\nThe worker processes tasks from `tasks/tasks-<containerName>.json` sequentially. For each task,\nit passes all subtasks to Claude as a single prompt and checks for a `<DONE>` signal.\n\n- If `<DONE>` is signaled: the task is dequeued and the worker moves to the next task.\n- If `<DONE>` is NOT signaled: the task is retried (up to 3 times before being skipped).\n\nThe worker commits changes after each task automatically. Focus on completing the\nsubtasks — do not worry about committing or exiting.\n\nWhen you have completed ALL subtasks in your task, output `<DONE>` to signal completion.\n\n## Task System\n\nWork is managed through a JSON task queue at `tasks/tasks-<containerName>.json`. The file contains an object\nwith a `tasks` array. Each task has a `skill`, an array of `subtasks` (description strings),\na `timestamp`, and an optional `app` name:\n\n```json\n{\n  \"tasks\": [\n    {\n      \"skill\": \"skills/tasks/maintain/checkDirectives.md\",\n      \"subtasks\": [\n        \"CheckTestSpecAuth: Check testSpec.md directive violations in Authentication\",\n        \"CheckComponentsAuth: Check writeApp.md directive violations in Authentication\",\n        \"CheckTestsAuth: Check writeTests.md directive violations in Authentication\"\n      ],\n      \"timestamp\": \"2026-02-20T00:00:00.000Z\",\n      \"app\": \"sales-crm\"\n    }\n  ]\n}\n```\n\nThe agent NEVER reads or writes task files directly. Instead, use `add-task` via stdin heredoc:\n\n```bash\nnpx tsx /repo/scripts/add-task.ts <<'EOF'\n[\n  { \"skill\": \"skills/tasks/build/writeApp.md\", \"app\": \"SalesCRM\", \"subtasks\": [\"SetupApp: Setup the app\", \"DesignDatabase: Design the database\"] },\n  { \"skill\": \"skills/tasks/build/writeTests.md\", \"app\": \"SalesCRM\", \"subtasks\": [\"WriteTestAuth: Write test for Auth\"] }\n]\nEOF\n```\n\nTasks are inserted at the front of the queue in the order listed (first element = first to run).\nEach task object must have `skill` and `subtasks`. `app` is optional.\n\n**CRITICAL: `add-task` usage rules**\n\n- When unpacking tasks, put ALL new tasks in a **single `add-task` call**. Do NOT call\n  `add-task` multiple times.\n- Write out every task explicitly in the JSON array. Do NOT use loops, scripts, or code\n  to generate tasks programmatically.\n- All subtasks in a task share the same skill. Group related subtasks together — for example,\n  all checks for a single page go in one task.\n- When a skill needs to \"unpack\" into sub-tasks, use `add-task` to insert them at the\n  front of the queue.\n\n**CRITICAL: Task scope rules**\n\n- You MUST only work on subtasks that were assigned to you in your current prompt. Your assigned\n  subtasks are the ones passed to you by the worker — nothing else.\n- When you add new tasks via `add-task`, you MUST NOT start working on those tasks.\n  They will be picked up by a future worker iteration.\n- If you have completed all of your assigned subtasks and the only remaining work is tasks you\n  added to the queue, you are DONE. Commit your changes and output `<DONE>` immediately.\n  Do NOT continue working on the newly queued tasks.\n- Adding tasks to the queue is NOT the same as doing those tasks. Add them and stop.\n\n## Tech Stack\n\n- **Vite** for build tooling\n- **TypeScript** for all source code (strict mode)\n- **React 18** for UI (single-page application)\n- **Redux** for client side state management\n- **shadcn/ui** for components\n- **Neon** for database backend (Postgres).\n- **Netlify Functions** for any backend/serverless functions. Place functions in `netlify/functions/`. Use `netlify dev` for local development.\n- **File Storage** use UploadThing with the provided token for any file storage.\n\n## Database\n\nAll database accesses must happen in backend Netlify functions. Netlify functions should be focused\nand operate on specific parts of the database corresponding to the needs of one or more specific\nfrontend components.\n\n## Running Tests\n\nYou MUST read `skills/tasks/build/testing.md` and precisely follow its instructions when running\ntests and debugging test failures.\n\n## Quality Gates\n\nBefore each commit, run `npm run check` (see `skills/scripts/check.md`). Do not commit\ncode that fails typecheck or lint.\n\n## Commits\n\nThe worker commits automatically after each iteration. Do not commit manually unless\nexplicitly asked to by a skill or prompt.\n","numLines":137,"startLine":1,"totalLines":137}}}
